            <ArticleTitle>The bee sting that was not: an unusual case of hymenoptera anaphylaxis averted in a patient treated with omalizumab for asthma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>138963</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1155/2014/138963</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This paper presents a case of hymenoptera venom anaphylaxis averted by omalizumab, a monoclonal antibody to IgE antibody. This case suggests a novel and unintentional effect of this therapy. Currently omalizumab is only FDA approved for the treatment of moderate-persistent allergic asthma. However case reports, such as ours have illustrated omalizumab's efficacy in the treatment of a myriad immunologic and allergic diseases. These outcomes have broadened the understanding of omalizumab's complex mechanism of action.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
